String views: read-only runs of code units that a view does not own, with Python's
read-only str methods. `ca::sv::str` (char, UTF-8 by convention) is the everyday one; every
code unit type has its own.

| View | Units |
|---|---|
| `sv::str` | char, UTF-8 by convention |
| `sv::u8` (`sv::utf8`) | char8_t |
| `sv::u16` (`sv::utf16`) | char16_t |
| `sv::u32` (`sv::utf32`) | char32_t, one per code point |
| `sv::wide` | wchar_t |
| `sv::basic<C>` | any of them, for generic code |

A view is two words, copied in registers, and is not NUL-terminated. It converts implicitly
from a C string (a literal included, its NUL excluded), and every string type of layer 5
(`ca::str`, `inline_str`) converts to it, so a function taking `sv::str` accepts all of them.
Sizes and indices count code units; `code_point_count()` is Python's `len()`.

```c++
#include "ca/sv.hpp"

static_assert(ca::sv::str{"  key = value  "}.strip() == ca::sv::str{"key = value"});
static_assert(ca::sv::str{"report.tar.gz"}.find_last('.').value_or(0U) == 10U);
static_assert(ca::sv::str{"report.tar.gz"}.strip_suffix(".gz") == ca::sv::str{"report.tar"});
static_assert(ca::sv::str{"banana"}.count("an") == 2U);
static_assert(ca::sv::str{"Hello"}.slice(1U, 3U) == ca::sv::str{"ell"});
static_assert(ca::sv::str{"ABC"}.is_upper() && ca::sv::str{"x9"}.is_alnum());
static_assert(ca::sv::str{"café"}.size() == 5U);
static_assert(ca::sv::str{"café"}.code_point_count() == 4U);
```

### Splitting and partitioning

`split`, `split_lines` and `partition` follow Python's rules exactly, and allocate nothing:
`split` returns a lazy range computing each piece as the loop advances.

- `split()` cuts on runs of white space and drops empty pieces.
- `split(sep)` keeps empty pieces, and yields one empty piece for empty text.
- `split(sep, max)` cuts at most max times, leaving the rest whole in the last piece.
- `split_lines()` cuts on every line boundary ("\r\n" counts as one); `split_lines(true)` keeps
  the boundaries.
- `partition(sep)` and `partition_last(sep)` give the text before, the separator and the text
  after; when the separator is missing, `found()` is false.

```c++
#include "ca/sv.hpp"

auto main(void) -> int {
	const ca::sv::str line{"name=ada,age=36,,lang=en"};
	int fields = 0;
	int empty = 0;
	for (const ca::sv::str field : line.split(",")) {
		++fields;
		if (field.empty())
			++empty;
	}
	if (fields != 4 || empty != 1)
		return 1;

	const auto kv = ca::sv::str{"age=36"}.partition("=");
	if (!kv.found() || kv.before() != ca::sv::str{"age"} || kv.after() != ca::sv::str{"36"})
		return 2;

	int words = 0;
	for (const ca::sv::str word : ca::sv::str{"  the quick\tbrown \n fox "}.split()) {
		if (word.empty())
			return 3;
		++words;
	}
	if (words != 4)
		return 4;

	int lines = 0;
	for (const ca::sv::str l : ca::sv::str{"a\r\nb\nc"}.split_lines())
		lines += static_cast<int>(l.size());
	return lines == 3 ? 0 : 5;
}
```

`ca::collect` (layer 5) turns a split into a `dynamic_array` when the pieces must be kept.

### Names that differ from Python

The method set is Python's str where it fits, renamed to snake_case words: `lstrip` and
`rstrip` are `strip_start` and `strip_end`, `removeprefix` and `removesuffix` are
`strip_prefix` and `strip_suffix`, `rfind` is `find_last`, `rpartition` is `partition_last`,
`splitlines` is `split_lines`, and each `isX` is `is_X`. `find` returns an optional index
instead of -1. The allocating methods (`upper`, `replace`, `center`, `join`, ...) are on
`ca::basic_str`, since a view cannot hold their result.

Character classes (`is_alpha`, `is_space`, ...) and white space are decided per code point by
the tables of `ca::unicode`, so `split()` also cuts on non-ASCII spaces. `compare` orders by
unsigned code unit, which is code point order for UTF-8 and UTF-32. `operator[]` traps out of
range in every build; `get(i)` returns an optional.
